WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.559
Welcome to Module 2 of Defender
X D R Engineering and Operations:

2
00:00:04.559 --> 00:00:07.537
prevention and Attack Surface Reduction.

3
00:00:07.537 --> 00:00:11.610
A setting in a policy protects nothing
until it's enforced on the device,

4
00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:15.475
and enforcing it all at once breaks
the applications nobody listed.

5
00:00:15.475 --> 00:00:19.021
In this module you learn the
order of the prevention settings,

6
00:00:19.021 --> 00:00:23.749
which of them have an audit mode, how Attack
Surface Reduction moves through audit,

7
00:00:23.749 --> 00:00:26.460
warn and block, ring by
ring, and how to prove each

8
00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.450
setting on the device rather
than trusting a report.

9
00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:34.700
The module covers Steps 3 and 4 of the
Defender X D R implementation guide.

10
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:39.230
It takes about fifty minutes, plus
Lab 2 in your own test tenant.

11
00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:44.530
Why does this module matter?

12
00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.205
The guide separates
configured from enforced.

13
00:00:47.205 --> 00:00:50.192
Configured is a setting
in an Intune policy.

14
00:00:50.192 --> 00:00:54.206
Audit mode is configured, and it
logs, but it protects nothing.

15
00:00:54.206 --> 00:00:58.017
And on Windows Server, Microsoft
lists a known issue where an Attack

16
00:00:58.017 --> 00:01:01.830
Surface Reduction rule reports
compliant without being enforced.

17
00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:06.464
Enforced is block mode on the ring,
after the ring's exit criteria are met,

18
00:01:06.464 --> 00:01:08.898
proved on the device and in the events,

19
00:01:08.898 --> 00:01:12.668
with every exclusion recorded
with an owner and an expiry.

20
00:01:12.668 --> 00:01:15.962
Getting there safely is
the work of this module:

21
00:01:15.962 --> 00:01:19.593
audit first, then enforce ring
by ring, with a way back.

22
00:01:19.593 --> 00:01:24.292
The guide counts Gates 3 and 4 as the
closure map's endpoint hardening row:

23
00:01:24.292 --> 00:01:28.757
prevention baselines and Attack
Surface Reduction by staged rings,

24
00:01:28.757 --> 00:01:32.020
evidenced by policy
and endpoint telemetry.

25
00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:38.683
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

26
00:01:38.683 --> 00:01:42.807
Name the prevention settings that have
an audit mode, and the ones that don't.

27
00:01:42.807 --> 00:01:47.685
Move network protection and controlled
folder access from audit to block,

28
00:01:47.685 --> 00:01:51.146
ring by ring, with exit
criteria and backout triggers.

29
00:01:51.146 --> 00:01:55.627
Run every Attack Surface Reduction rule
through audit and warn into block,

30
00:01:55.627 --> 00:01:57.795
and name the two rules that skip warn.

31
00:01:57.795 --> 00:02:02.445
Record per-rule exclusions, and
prefer an exclusion to a step back.

32
00:02:02.445 --> 00:02:07.257
And verify each setting on the device, and
read the fleet's audit and block events.

33
00:02:07.257 --> 00:02:10.720
You need Module 1, so the
ring's devices are onboarded.

34
00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:15.567
Skim the Devices and Endpoints book's
policies E S oh-oh-one for antivirus,

35
00:02:15.567 --> 00:02:20.656
E S oh-oh-three for the firewall, and E S
oh-oh-five for Attack Surface Reduction:

36
00:02:20.656 --> 00:02:23.645
the setting values live
there, not in the guide.

37
00:02:23.645 --> 00:02:27.360
Lab 2 runs in a test
tenant, never production.

38
00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.447
Unit 1 is prevention through audit.

39
00:02:33.447 --> 00:02:37.748
Antivirus, firewall and tamper
protection carry the values from the

40
00:02:37.748 --> 00:02:42.198
Devices and Endpoints book's policy
library, and they have no audit mode.

41
00:02:42.198 --> 00:02:46.522
They go straight to ring one, with
backout triggers set before the change.

42
00:02:46.522 --> 00:02:49.461
Two settings do have an
audit mode, value two.

43
00:02:49.461 --> 00:02:54.690
Microsoft Learn says network protection
in audit logs the connections it would

44
00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:57.969
block, as event eleven
twenty-five, and in block mode,

45
00:02:57.969 --> 00:03:00.935
value one, the block is
event eleven twenty-six.

46
00:03:00.935 --> 00:03:06.266
Controlled folder access in audit logs
untrusted writes to protected folders, event

47
00:03:06.266 --> 00:03:11.072
eleven twenty-four, and blocks them in
block mode as event eleven twenty-three.

48
00:03:11.072 --> 00:03:15.627
Network protection has prerequisites:
Defender Antivirus in active mode,

49
00:03:15.627 --> 00:03:19.960
behavior monitoring, and cloud
protection with working connectivity.

50
00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.569
On Windows Server twenty
nineteen and later,

51
00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:27.010
it also needs the setting that allows
network protection on Windows Server.

52
00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:32.096
And on servers with heavy U D P traffic,
such as domain controllers, D N S,

53
00:03:32.096 --> 00:03:37.720
file and sequel servers, Learn says
to set datagram processing to false.

54
00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:42.633
Then enforcement.

55
00:03:42.633 --> 00:03:46.133
The pilot runs on rings zero
and one for at least two weeks,

56
00:03:46.133 --> 00:03:50.081
with network protection and
controlled folder access in audit;

57
00:03:50.081 --> 00:03:53.507
that length is the program's
risk-based recommendation.

58
00:03:53.507 --> 00:03:56.336
Read the audit events
every week and add allowed

59
00:03:56.336 --> 00:04:00.083
applications for controlled
folder access before you enforce.

60
00:04:00.083 --> 00:04:04.483
Then turn each setting to block in
the Intune policy, ring by ring.

61
00:04:04.483 --> 00:04:09.092
Ring zero, the implementing team, exits
when the audit events are reviewed,

62
00:04:09.092 --> 00:04:12.720
the test site is blocked and no
one has called the help desk.

63
00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.454
Ring one, the pilot
group, after two weeks,

64
00:04:15.454 --> 00:04:20.507
when every audited line-of-business
block has an allow entry or a decision.

65
00:04:20.507 --> 00:04:24.325
Ring two, early adopters, after
one week, with help-desk calls

66
00:04:24.325 --> 00:04:28.532
tagged Defender block under the
threshold set before the change.

67
00:04:28.532 --> 00:04:30.484
Ring three, everyone in scope,

68
00:04:30.484 --> 00:04:34.470
when post-change validation
passes on a sample at each site.

69
00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:39.186
The backout triggers are a blocked
critical application with no allow path,

70
00:04:39.186 --> 00:04:41.582
or help-desk volume over the threshold.

71
00:04:41.582 --> 00:04:44.920
Rollback sets the mode back
to audit in the same policy.

72
00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:47.552
Microsoft Learn says tamper protection is

73
00:04:47.552 --> 00:04:50.550
turned off only from
the console that set it.

74
00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.825
Unit 2 is Attack Surface Reduction.

75
00:04:56.825 --> 00:05:01.038
Microsoft Learn's rule modes
are zero for off, one for block,

76
00:05:01.038 --> 00:05:04.900
two for audit, five for not
configured, and six for warn.

77
00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:07.728
Before you audit, Learn
says to turn off any

78
00:05:07.728 --> 00:05:10.637
related rule that's
already in block or warn.

79
00:05:10.637 --> 00:05:13.590
Then put every rule in
audit at once on ring one,

80
00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:18.174
which Learn recommends, to see which
rules everyday business triggers.

81
00:05:18.174 --> 00:05:21.425
The program's stages are
risk-based recommendations.

82
00:05:21.425 --> 00:05:23.867
Audit on ring one for thirty days,

83
00:05:23.867 --> 00:05:28.662
until every audited rule hit has
an exclusion, a fix or a decision.

84
00:05:28.662 --> 00:05:33.040
Then warn, first on ring one and
then on ring two, for fourteen days,

85
00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:36.839
counting unblock requests,
until no business-critical

86
00:05:36.839 --> 00:05:40.225
application is left without
a per-rule exclusion.

87
00:05:40.225 --> 00:05:43.822
Then block, ring by ring,
until blocks per rule per week

88
00:05:43.822 --> 00:05:47.500
are stable and help-desk
volume is under the threshold.

89
00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:50.452
Microsoft's own position,
from its rules overview,

90
00:05:50.452 --> 00:05:55.277
is that the standard protection rules can
go to block without extensive testing,

91
00:05:55.277 --> 00:05:57.725
and other rules are tested in audit first.

92
00:05:57.725 --> 00:06:02.110
The program runs all three
stages anyway, and says so.

93
00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:09.123
Warn shows the user the
block, with a way to proceed.

94
00:06:09.123 --> 00:06:13.690
Microsoft Learn's rules overview
says warn needs Windows ten, version

95
00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:18.810
eighteen oh nine or later, and a recent
Defender Antivirus platform and engine.

96
00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:21.708
It isn't available in
Configuration Manager,

97
00:06:21.708 --> 00:06:24.998
and on older Windows a rule
set to warn acts as block.

98
00:06:24.998 --> 00:06:26.885
Warn isn't a safety valve, though.

99
00:06:26.885 --> 00:06:30.582
Since platform four point
eighteen point two six oh six oh,

100
00:06:30.582 --> 00:06:35.281
using Unblock on a warn rule needs
administrator approval, and Microsoft

101
00:06:35.281 --> 00:06:40.210
says a persistent need belongs in a
per-rule exclusion, not repeated unblocks.

102
00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:42.805
So brief the help desk
before the warn ring;

103
00:06:42.805 --> 00:06:47.105
the guide's RACI makes the help desk
responsible for unblock requests

104
00:06:47.105 --> 00:06:50.960
and user impact, and the policy
owner accountable for the rings.

105
00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:55.235
Two rules don't support warn at all,
and go from audit straight to block.

106
00:06:55.235 --> 00:07:00.198
One blocks credential stealing from the
Windows local security authority subsystem.

107
00:07:00.198 --> 00:07:05.320
The other blocks Office applications from
injecting code into other processes.

108
00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:12.045
Unit 3 is exclusions, and the traps.

109
00:07:12.045 --> 00:07:16.327
When a rule in block breaks an
application, Microsoft's advice in Learn

110
00:07:16.327 --> 00:07:20.682
is that an exclusion is better than
turning the rule off or back to audit.

111
00:07:20.682 --> 00:07:25.740
Per-rule exclusions go in the same policy,
under A S R only per rule exclusions,

112
00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:29.111
as a file path rather than
a folder wherever you can,

113
00:07:29.111 --> 00:07:33.045
and each one goes in the register
with an owner and an expiry.

114
00:07:33.045 --> 00:07:36.277
Learn adds that if Disable
Local Admin Merge is true,

115
00:07:36.277 --> 00:07:38.607
local per-rule exclusions don't apply.

116
00:07:38.607 --> 00:07:42.714
Rollback, when you need it,
moves a rule one stage back:

117
00:07:42.714 --> 00:07:45.007
block to warn, or warn to audit.

118
00:07:45.007 --> 00:07:46.245
Then the dangers.

119
00:07:46.245 --> 00:07:50.265
Configure a rule in Intune or
in Group Policy, never both:

120
00:07:50.265 --> 00:07:55.683
Learn says Group Policy wins by default,
so the Intune value silently loses.

121
00:07:55.683 --> 00:07:59.800
And on Windows Server, Learn lists
a known issue, with no fix date,

122
00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:03.283
where a rule shows compliant
without being enforced.

123
00:08:03.283 --> 00:08:07.029
So on servers, trust the
device-side check and the events,

124
00:08:07.029 --> 00:08:09.180
not the compliance report.

125
00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:17.466
Lab 2 runs the guide's validate
commands on a ring zero test device,

126
00:08:17.466 --> 00:08:19.818
in a test tenant, never production.

127
00:08:19.818 --> 00:08:22.818
The authors haven't run
them, as the page says.

128
00:08:22.818 --> 00:08:26.963
You need Module 1's test tenant and
test device, with the antivirus,

129
00:08:26.963 --> 00:08:32.105
firewall and Attack Surface Reduction
policies assigned to its ring zero group.

130
00:08:32.105 --> 00:08:36.055
Steps one to five run on the
device, in an elevated session.

131
00:08:36.055 --> 00:08:39.150
Step one shows tamper
and real-time protection;

132
00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:41.768
both should be true, which means enabled.

133
00:08:41.768 --> 00:08:47.083
Step two shows the network protection
and controlled folder access modes:

134
00:08:47.083 --> 00:08:50.855
zero off, one block, two audit,
matching the ring's stage.

135
00:08:50.855 --> 00:08:54.268
Step three shows the firewall
profiles as enforced,

136
00:08:54.268 --> 00:08:56.967
with the values the firewall policy sets.

137
00:08:56.967 --> 00:09:00.637
Step four is Microsoft Learn's
network protection test:

138
00:09:00.637 --> 00:09:04.467
in a browser other than Edge,
open the test site on the slide.

139
00:09:04.467 --> 00:09:08.965
In block mode the connection is blocked
with a notification, event eleven

140
00:09:08.965 --> 00:09:13.242
twenty-six; in audit it isn't blocked,
and the event is eleven twenty-five.

141
00:09:13.242 --> 00:09:17.769
Step five is Learn's command that
lists each rule and its mode:

142
00:09:17.769 --> 00:09:20.667
two for audit, six for
warn, one for block.

143
00:09:20.667 --> 00:09:23.690
On a server, this is the check to trust.

144
00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:32.940
Steps six to eight run on your workstation,
through Graph, so each result is a file.

145
00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:37.278
The slide shortens them to fit;
the page has every command in full.

146
00:09:37.278 --> 00:09:41.872
Step six connects and counts the fleet's
prevention events over seven days:

147
00:09:41.872 --> 00:09:44.392
network protection audited and blocked,

148
00:09:44.392 --> 00:09:48.765
controlled folder access audited
and blocked, and tampering attempts.

149
00:09:48.765 --> 00:09:51.190
Step four's test should appear among them.

150
00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:54.349
Step seven is Microsoft
Learn's query for Attack

151
00:09:54.349 --> 00:09:57.665
Surface Reduction events
by rule over thirty days.

152
00:09:57.665 --> 00:10:01.062
Each rule has its own audited
and blocked action types,

153
00:10:01.062 --> 00:10:05.440
and Learn says the events are throttled
to unique processes each hour.

154
00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:08.609
Step eight is Learn's pattern
for one rule's detail,

155
00:10:08.609 --> 00:10:12.782
here the Office child-process rule:
the devices, files and command

156
00:10:12.782 --> 00:10:17.265
lines it audited or blocked, which
is what an exclusion decision reads.

157
00:10:17.265 --> 00:10:19.477
Step nine is the only write.

158
00:10:19.477 --> 00:10:23.477
It's the guide's rollback for a
single device under investigation:

159
00:10:23.477 --> 00:10:26.440
Learn's local command sets
one rule back to audit.

160
00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:29.490
Step five's command now
shows the rule at two.

161
00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:33.460
Intune overwrites the local
value at the next policy refresh,

162
00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:36.690
and that's the back-out;
nothing else is needed.

163
00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:43.752
The federal requirement, from
the guide's requirements table.

164
00:10:43.752 --> 00:10:49.615
Gate three supports four controls from
NIST special publication eight hundred

165
00:10:49.615 --> 00:10:54.777
fifty-three, revision five: S I three,
malicious code protection; S C seven,

166
00:10:54.777 --> 00:10:58.452
boundary protection, here
the host firewall; C M six,

167
00:10:58.452 --> 00:11:03.702
configuration settings; and S I seven,
integrity, here tamper protection.

168
00:11:03.702 --> 00:11:08.790
The evidence is the policy exports, the
device checks and the audit results.

169
00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:15.365
Gate four supports C M seven, least
functionality, with C M six and S I three.

170
00:11:15.365 --> 00:11:17.957
The evidence is the rule-by-ring matrix,

171
00:11:17.957 --> 00:11:21.927
with the date each rule entered
block, and the per-rule tests.

172
00:11:21.927 --> 00:11:26.034
Each row lines up with fed-ramp
twenty X key security indicators,

173
00:11:26.034 --> 00:11:31.303
which bind cloud service providers; the
agency lines its own evidence up with them.

174
00:11:31.303 --> 00:11:33.393
The gates support these controls,

175
00:11:33.393 --> 00:11:36.970
and the assessor decides
whether each is satisfied.

176
00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:42.695
Check yourself with two questions.

177
00:11:42.695 --> 00:11:47.783
First: which two Attack Surface Reduction
rules go from audit straight to block?

178
00:11:47.783 --> 00:11:52.300
The answer is a: the rule that blocks
credential stealing from the local security

179
00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:55.739
authority, and the rule that
blocks Office applications from

180
00:11:55.739 --> 00:11:59.582
injecting code into other processes,
because neither supports warn.

181
00:11:59.582 --> 00:12:03.883
Second: a Windows Server shows
a rule as compliant in Intune.

182
00:12:03.883 --> 00:12:05.282
Is that enough evidence?

183
00:12:05.282 --> 00:12:06.821
The answer is b, no.

184
00:12:06.821 --> 00:12:11.603
Microsoft lists a known issue where the
server shows compliant without the rule

185
00:12:11.603 --> 00:12:14.244
being enforced, so check
on the server itself,

186
00:12:14.244 --> 00:12:16.457
with Get M P Preference and the events.

187
00:12:16.457 --> 00:12:20.608
Setting the rule in Group Policy
too would only add the other danger.

188
00:12:20.608 --> 00:12:25.870
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

189
00:12:29.620 --> 00:12:30.445
To sum up.

190
00:12:30.445 --> 00:12:34.883
You checked every prevention setting
and every rule on a test device.

191
00:12:34.883 --> 00:12:38.194
You triggered Microsoft Learn's
network protection test.

192
00:12:38.194 --> 00:12:40.806
You filed the fleet's
audit and block events,

193
00:12:40.806 --> 00:12:43.708
and its Attack Surface
Reduction events by rule.

194
00:12:43.708 --> 00:12:48.395
And you stepped one rule back on one
device, the way the guide's rollback does.

195
00:12:48.395 --> 00:12:52.292
The evidence an I S S O keeps
from this module: the antivirus,

196
00:12:52.292 --> 00:12:56.919
firewall and Attack Surface Reduction
policy exports for each ring;

197
00:12:56.919 --> 00:12:59.680
the weekly audit and block query results;

198
00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:04.307
the network protection test and the
tamper status; the per-rule tests;

199
00:13:04.307 --> 00:13:08.366
the rule-by-ring matrix, with
the date each rule entered block;

200
00:13:08.366 --> 00:13:11.695
and every exclusion, with
its approver and expiry.

201
00:13:11.695 --> 00:13:14.603
Next is Module 3, exposure and incidents,

202
00:13:14.603 --> 00:13:20.740
where every vulnerability gets an owner
and every incident gets triaged to a table.
